# Logical Operators

In the last lesson we produced booleans by comparing two values. **Logical operators** let us combine those booleans into larger, more nuanced ones. There are three:

- `&&` (logical AND)
- `||` (logical OR)
- `!` (logical NOT)

Throughout this lesson, imagine two expressions `a` and `b` whose values are booleans. They might be the literals `true` and `false`, but far more often they are comparisons like `x > y` and `z === 5`.

## Logical AND (`&&`)

The AND operator is two ampersands. The expression `a && b` is `true` only when `a` is `true` **and** `b` is `true`. If either side is `false`, the whole thing is `false`.

That is easiest to see as a **truth table**, which lists every combination the two sides can have:

| `a`     | `b`     | `a && b` |
|---------|---------|----------|
| `true`  | `true`  | `true`   |
| `true`  | `false` | `false`  |
| `false` | `true`  | `false`  |
| `false` | `false` | `false`  |

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(true && true);
console.log(true && false);
console.log(false && true);
console.log(false && false);
```

In real code the two sides are usually comparisons. Checking that a number falls inside a range takes two comparisons joined with `&&`:

```{javascript autorun}
const num = 15;

console.log(num > 10 && num < 20);
```

`num > 10` is `true` and `num < 20` is `true`, so the whole expression is `true`. Change `num` to `25` and run it again: the second comparison becomes `false`, so the result does too.

## Logical OR (`||`)

The OR operator is two vertical bars. The expression `a || b` is `true` when `a` is `true`, or `b` is `true`, or both. It is `false` only when both sides are `false`.

| `a`     | `b`     | `a \|\| b` |
|---------|---------|------------|
| `true`  | `true`  | `true`     |
| `true`  | `false` | `true`     |
| `false` | `true`  | `true`     |
| `false` | `false` | `false`    |

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(true || true);
console.log(true || false);
console.log(false || true);
console.log(false || false);
```

A natural use is "either of these is good enough":

```{javascript autorun}
const isWeekend = false;
const isHoliday = true;

const canRelax = isWeekend || isHoliday;

console.log(canRelax);
```

It is not the weekend, but it is a holiday, so `canRelax` is `true`.

## Logical NOT (`!`)

The NOT operator is an exclamation point, and unlike the other two it takes only one expression. `!a` is the opposite of `a`.

| `a`     | `!a`    |
|---------|---------|
| `true`  | `false` |
| `false` | `true`  |

```{javascript autorun}
const isHot = true;
const isCold = !isHot;

console.log(isCold);
console.log(!(1 === 2));
```

The last line is worth a look. `1 === 2` is `false`, so `!(1 === 2)` is `true`. The parentheses matter: they make the comparison happen first, so that `!` has a boolean to flip.

## Combining More Than Two

Nothing stops us from chaining these together, and this is where the "more nuanced" part comes in. Suppose we want to know whether a letter is a vowel:

```{javascript autorun}
const letter = 'a';

const isA = letter === 'a';
const isE = letter === 'e';
const isI = letter === 'i';
const isO = letter === 'o';
const isU = letter === 'u';

const isVowel = isA || isE || isI || isO || isU;
const isConsonant = !isVowel;

console.log(isVowel);
console.log(isConsonant);
```

`isVowel` is `true` if *any* of the five comparisons is true, so the five are joined with `||`. And a letter is a consonant exactly when it is not a vowel, which is what `!isVowel` says. Change `letter` to `'m'` and run it again; both values flip.

Note what would happen if we had written `&&` instead of `||` in `isVowel`. No letter can be `a` and `e` at the same time, so the expression could never be true. Choosing the wrong one of these two operators produces a condition that is always wrong rather than an error, which is why it pays to read them out loud: "a or e or i or o or u".

Here is a more realistic one. Imagine a site where people sign up and we want to know whether a chosen username is acceptable:

```{javascript autorun}
const username = 'soney';

const isLongEnough = username.length > 3;
const isAlreadyTaken = false;
const hasSpecialCharacters = false;

const isValid = isLongEnough && !isAlreadyTaken && !hasSpecialCharacters;

console.log(isValid);
```

`username.length` gives the number of characters in a string, so `isLongEnough` checks that the name is longer than three characters. `isValid` is `true` only when the name is long enough **and** is not already taken **and** has no special characters. In a real site the last two would be worked out by code that checks a list of existing users; we will learn to write that kind of code when we get to functions in a later lesson.

So logical operators let us combine boolean expressions into ones that say exactly what we mean. You might reasonably ask what we can do with a boolean other than log it. That is the next lesson: **conditional execution**, where a boolean decides which parts of our code run at all.

## Exercises

1. Write an expression for `isTeenager` that is `true` when `age` is at least `13` and at most `19`.

    ```{javascript}
    const age = 15;

    const isTeenager = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(isTeenager);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const age = 15;

    const isTeenager = age >= 13 && age <= 19;

    console.log(isTeenager);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(isTeenager).should('equal', true).run('`isTeenager` should be `true` when `age` is 15', 'isTeenager is true for 15');
    wrap(source.includes('&&')).should('equal', true).run('Combine two comparisons with `&&`', 'Used the `&&` operator');
    ```

2. Write an expression for `needsCoat` that is `true` when it is either cold or raining.

    ```{javascript}
    const isCold = false;
    const isRaining = true;

    const needsCoat = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(needsCoat);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const isCold = false;
    const isRaining = true;

    const needsCoat = isCold || isRaining;

    console.log(needsCoat);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(needsCoat).should('equal', true).run('`needsCoat` should be `true` when it is raining, even though it is not cold', 'needsCoat is true');
    wrap(source.includes('||')).should('equal', true).run('Combine the two booleans with `||`', 'Used the `||` operator');
    ```

3. Negating a comparison

    ```{mcq}
    The expression `!(5 > 3)` is evaluated. What is its value?
    correct: Correct! `5 > 3` is `true`, and `!` flips it, so the whole expression is `false`.
    wrong: Work the expression from the inside out: first decide what `5 > 3` is, then apply the `!`.
    [x] `false`
    [ ] `true`
    [ ] `5`
    [ ] It causes an error, because `!` only works on the literals `true` and `false`
    ```

